introduction:
  bigQuestion: "What actually happens inside the box?"
  overview: "This unit peels back the layers of abstraction to reveal the physical reality of the <strong>Von Neumann Architecture</strong>. You will learn how the <strong>CPU</strong> orchestrates billions of operations per second using the <strong>Fetch-Decode-Execute</strong> cycle, and how memory hierarchies balance speed, cost, and capacity."
  journey: "You'll start by mapping the internal topography of the <strong>CPU</strong> (Registers, ALU, CU). Then you'll trace the journey of a single instruction through the <strong>FDE Cycle</strong>, optimizing performance with <strong>Cache</strong> and <strong>Secondary Storage</strong>, before expanding to the global scale of <strong>Cloud Computing</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "The CPU & Registers"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the brain. It contains specific components:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit):</strong> Does the math and logic checks.</li>
          <li><strong>CU (Control Unit):</strong> The conductor that directs data flow.</li>
          <li><strong>Registers:</strong> Tiny, super-fast storage slots (e.g., PC, MAR, MDR, ACC).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Memory Hierarchy"
    icon: "fa-memory"
    content: |
      <p>Not all memory is created equal. We balance speed vs size:</p>
      <ol>
          <li><strong>Registers:</strong> Fastest, Tiny size.</li>
          <li><strong>Cache (L1/L2/L3):</strong> Very fast, Small size.</li>
          <li><strong>RAM (Primary):</strong> Fast, Medium size (Volatile).</li>
          <li><strong>Storage (Secondary):</strong> Slow, Huge size (Non-volatile, e.g., SSD).</li>
      </ol>

  - title: "The FDE Cycle"
    icon: "fa-rotate"
    content: |
      <p>The heartbeat of the computer. Millions of times a second, it follows these steps:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Fetch:</strong> Get the next instruction from RAM.</li>
          <li><strong>Decode:</strong> Figure out what it means.</li>
          <li><strong>Execute:</strong> Do it (e.g., Add numbers, Store data).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Cloud Computing"
    icon: "fa-cloud"
    content: |
      <p>"The Cloud" is just someone else's computer. We abstract infrastructure using service models:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>SaaS:</strong> Software as a Service (e.g., Google Docs).</li>
          <li><strong>PaaS:</strong> Platform as a Service (e.g., Heroku).</li>
          <li><strong>IaaS:</strong> Infrastructure as a Service (e.g., AWS EC2).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Von Neumann Architecture"
    definition: "A design where data and instructions are stored in the same memory and accessed via buses."
  - term: "Program Counter (PC)"
    definition: "A register that holds the memory address of the NEXT instruction to be executed."
  - term: "MAR (Memory Address Register)"
    definition: "Holds the address of the memory location currently being accessed (read or written)."
  - term: "MDR (Memory Data Register)"
    definition: "Holds the data that has just been fetched from, or is about to be written to, memory."
  - term: "Accumulator (ACC)"
    definition: "A register that holds the intermediate results of ALU calculations."
  - term: "Cache"
    definition: "Small, high-speed memory located near the CPU to store frequently used data."
  - term: "Primary Memory (RAM)"
    definition: "Volatile memory that holds the OS, programs, and data currently in use."
  - term: "Secondary Storage"
    definition: "Non-volatile storage (HDD/SSD) used to allow data to be persistent."
  - term: "SaaS / PaaS / IaaS"
    definition: "Service models for Cloud Computing (Software / Platform / Infrastructure as a Service)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Memory vs Storage"
    correction: "Thinking a Hard Drive is 'Memory'. RAM is Memory (fast/volatile workspace). Hard Drive is Storage (slow/permanent filing cabinet)."
  - mistake: "The Cloud is Virtual"
    correction: "Thinking the cloud is magic ether. It's actually a physical server farm (that uses electricity and generates heat!)."
  - mistake: "More Cores = Faster"
    correction: "Assuming 4 cores is 4x speed. Many programs can't be split (parallelized), so single-core speed often matters more."

